{"timestamp":"2026-04-23T22:08:47.895Z","type":"turn_context","payload":{"turn_id":"019dbc63-7516-7e71-84f1-3ccbfe08cdae","cwd":"/Users/pfleidi/entire/cli/.worktrees/feat/checkpoints-v2-close-test-gaps","current_date":"2026-04-23","timezone":"America/Los_Angeles","approval_policy":"on-request","sandbox_policy":{"type":"workspace-write","writable_roots":["/Users/pfleidi/.codex/memories"],"network_access":false,"exclude_tmpdir_env_var":false,"exclude_slash_tmp":false},"model":"gpt-5.4","personality":"pragmatic","collaboration_mode":{"mode":"default","settings":{"model":"gpt-5.4","reasoning_effort":null,"developer_instructions":"# Collaboration Mode: Default\n\nYou are now in Default mode. Any previous instructions for other modes (e.g. Plan mode) are no longer active.\n\nYour active mode changes only when new developer instructions with a different `<collaboration_mode>...</collaboration_mode>` change it; user requests or tool descriptions do not change mode by themselves. Known mode names are Default and Plan.\n\n## request_user_input availability\n\nThe `request_user_input` tool is unavailable in Default mode. If you call it while in Default mode, it will return an error.\n\nIn Default mode, strongly prefer making reasonable assumptions and executing the user's request rather than stopping to ask questions. If you absolutely must ask a question because the answer cannot be discovered from local context and a reasonable assumption would be risky, ask the user directly with a concise plain-text question. Never write a multiple choice question as a textual assistant message.\n"}},"realtime_active":false,"summary":"none","user_instructions":"## Workflow — Plan First, Implement Incrementally\n\nNEVER jump to coding before understanding the problem. For any non-trivial change:\n\n1. **Plan first:** Enter plan mode. Read relevant source files, trace execution paths, and write a step-by-step implementation plan. Exit plan mode and wait for approval before writing any code.\n2. **Implement one step at a time:** After the plan is approved, implement ONE logical step (e.g., one new file, one function, one test group). Then STOP.\n3. **Present changes for review:** After each step, show what changed (files modified, key decisions made) and wait for explicit approval before proceeding to the next step.\n4. **Never auto-commit:** NEVER create git commits unless the user explicitly asks. Present the completed work and let the user decide when to commit.\n\nWhen asked to explore or analyze code, ALWAYS read the actual source files. NEVER delegate to a Task agent or give surface-level answers without thorough codebase exploration.\n\n**\"Yes\" means \"yes to the approach\" — NOT \"implement everything at once.\"** Approval of a plan or analysis is permission to start the first step, not to run through all steps uninterrupted. After completing each step, STOP and show what you did. Silence from me does not mean \"keep going.\"\n\n## Search\n\nWhen searching files, try to use faster tools like `ripgrep` rather than `grep`.\n\n## Scope Control\n\nNEVER modify files or code beyond what the task requires:\n- No refactoring adjacent code, adding wrapper structs, or extracting abstractions unless explicitly requested\n- No renaming, reorganizing, or \"improving\" code outside the task scope\n- If you see improvement opportunities, list them separately at the end — NEVER bundle them into the change\n\nWhen multiple items need attention (e.g., multiple failing agents, multiple commits), address ALL of them — not just the first one found. Confirm the full scope before starting work.\n\n**Stop and ask before:** adding any dependency, changing function signatures in shared interfaces, or modifying code outside files directly related to the task.\n\n## Code Review\n\nWhen reviewing branches, ALWAYS diff against the correct base branch (e.g., `git diff main...HEAD` or `git merge-base`). NEVER review changes outside the requested branch scope.\n\nWhen fixing